content.text:
Yes, exactly. Here's the specific line in `MergeService.java:55`:

```java
} else if (stored.getValue().equals(followUp.getValue())) {
    result.put(name, new MergedField(followUp, "unchanged", null));
}
```

Only `.getValue()` is compared. `confidence` and `source` are never part of the condition.

So if the stored field is:
```json
{ "value": "M.K.", "confidence": 0.98, "source": "p.2 §1" }
```

And the follow-up sends:
```json
{ "value": "M.K.", "confidence": 0.99, "source": "p.2 §1" }
```

The status is `"unchanged"` — because the values are identical — but the stored record will be updated with confidence `0.99` (the follow-up's metadata wins, since `new MergedField(followUp, ...)` takes the follow-up object).

The rationale is in the spec: *"Changes to metadata such as confidence or source should not affect the status."* Confidence and source are AI extraction artifacts that can improve between runs without the underlying fact changing. A reviewer shouldn't see a conflict just because the AI got more confident about the same answer.

